@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.1918 = private unnamed_addr constant [810 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for key-existence checks against `dict.keys()` calls.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen checking for the existence of a key in a given dictionary, using\0A`key in dict` is more readable and efficient than `key in dict.keys()`,\0Awhile having the same semantics.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Akey in foo.keys()\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Akey in foo\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AGiven `key in obj.keys()`, `obj` _could_ be a dictionary, or it could be\0Aanother type that defines a `.keys()` method. In the latter case, removing\0Athe `.keys()` attribute could lead to a runtime error. The fix is marked\0Aas safe when the type of `obj` is known to be a dictionary; otherwise, it\0Ais marked as unsafe.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: Mapping Types](https://docs.python.org/3/library/stdtypes.html#mapping-types-dict)\0A", align 1
