@anon.0de5ee8f4a9046f07397637c35d34c13.2196 = private unnamed_addr constant [861 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for list reversals that can be performed in-place in lieu of\0Acreating a new list.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AWhen reversing a list, it's more efficient to use the in-place method\0A`.reverse()` instead of creating a new list, if the original list is\0Ano longer needed.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Al = [1, 2, 3]\0Al = reversed(l)\0A\0Al = [1, 2, 3]\0Al = list(reversed(l))\0A\0Al = [1, 2, 3]\0Al = l[::-1]\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Al = [1, 2, 3]\0Al.reverse()\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as calling `.reverse()` on a list\0Awill mutate the list in-place, unlike `reversed`, which creates a new list\0Aand leaves the original list unchanged.\0A\0AIf the list is referenced elsewhere, this could lead to unexpected\0Abehavior.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: More on Lists](https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#more-on-lists)\0A", align 1
